# Caddy as the TLS reverse proxy in front of autobracket. Caddy gets and renews the
# certificate itself: point the name's DNS at this machine, open ports 80 and 443, and replace
# bracket.example.org with your own name. Players and the admin CLI then use
# https://bracket.example.org.
#
# autobracket rate-limits per client address, and behind a proxy every request comes from the
# proxy. Caddy sends the client's address in X-Forwarded-For; list the address Caddy connects
# from in the server's trusted_proxies so it is believed (README, "TLS and reverse proxies"):
#   Caddy and the server on the same host, the server not in Docker (e.g. systemd):
#     trusted_proxies = ["127.0.0.1/32"]   (`init --proxy-cidr 127.0.0.1/32`)
#   The server under deploy/docker-compose.yml, Caddy on the host proxying to its loopback
#   port: requests arrive from the pinned network's gateway:
#     trusted_proxies = ["10.203.47.1/32"]   (`init --proxy-cidr 10.203.47.1/32`)
# Never put a range in trusted_proxies that contains ANY client's address (a LAN, a VPN, a
# whole Docker subnet): a client whose own address is trusted can choose the address the
# server sees. Trust only the proxy's own address(es), as narrowly as possible (a /32).

bracket.example.org {
	# Replay uploads are up to 16 MiB (the server's max_replay_bytes); allow a little more.
	request_body {
		max_size 17MB
	}
	# Plain HTTP to the server; no WebSockets. Caddy sets X-Forwarded-For, -Proto and -Host.
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
}
